IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT domain
IMPORT `g6-interpretation`

-- g6-art20-court-orders.l4: Article 20 of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Court orders as to fitness of governor.
-- Split from the survey's single module `g6-art19-20-fitness` (see g6-art19-reportable-matters.l4): the order records defined here are read by Article 19(1)(b) and (10).
-- Inputs from other groups (PLAN section (c)): none beyond the standard types of domain.l4. Outputs: IP24 `Art 20(1)(c) the court orders that the entity must not be registered`
-- (read by G4 for Article 11(4)), IP30 `the penalty provided for the offence` (Article 20(6)), and the records of the order for Article 19.
-- The court "sees fit", "appears necessary or equitable" and "is satisfied": each is a determination the caller supplies; the content of an order (which charity, which conditions) is an input.

§ `Part 5 -- Governors of registered charities`

§§ `Article 20 -- Court orders as to fitness of governor`

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The nouns of this Article
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The kinds of order Article 20(1) names, and the open case ("any order that the court sees fit, including without limitation").
DECLARE `Kind of order under Article 20(1)` IS ONE OF
    `an order dismissing the application`
    `an order giving permission for the governor to act as a governor despite a reportable matter`
    `an order that the entity must not be registered as a charity`
    `an order requiring a registered charity to suspend or remove the governor`
    `an order making any provision that could be made by a required steps notice`
    `an order ancillary to any such order`
    `any other order that the court sees fit`

-- What an order may apply to (Article 20(2)(a)).
DECLARE `Reach of an order under Article 20(2)(a)` IS ONE OF
    `a particular registered charity`
    `a description of registered charities`
    `all registered charities`

-- An order the court has made on an application under Article 19(9)(b) or (13).
DECLARE `Order made by the court under Article 20(1)` HAS
    kind IS A `Kind of order under Article 20(1)`
        @desc Which of the orders of Article 20(1) it is
    `made on` IS A DATE
        @desc The day the order was made
    reach IS A `Reach of an order under Article 20(2)(a)`
        @desc Whether the order applies to a particular registered charity, to a description of charities, or to all
    `time limit ends on` IS A MAYBE DATE
        @desc The last day named in a time limit on the order (Article 20(2)(b)); NOTHING if it has none

-- A disqualification order (Article 20(3)): whom it binds and the days it runs. The period is the court's direction (Article 20(5) limits it).
DECLARE `Disqualification order` HAS
    person IS A STRING
        @desc The name of the person the order binds
    `begins on` IS A DATE
        @desc The first day of the period the court directs
    `last day of the period` IS A DATE
        @desc The last day on which the order applies

-- What a party does under Article 20, value-actor style.
DECLARE `Act under Article 20` IS ONE OF
    `make an order on the application` HAS by IS A `Legal actor`
                                         , kind IS A `Kind of order under Article 20(1)`
    `apply to the court for a disqualification order` HAS by IS A `Legal actor`
    `make a disqualification order` HAS by IS A `Legal actor`
    `be a governor of, or be concerned or take part in the management of, a registered charity` HAS by IS A `Legal actor`

§§§ `Article 20(1) -- the orders the court may make`

-- (1) On an application under Article 19(9)(b) or (13), the court may make any order that the court sees fit. One rule for the power, and one limb for each kind named.
-- src:514 | (1) On an application under Article 19(9)(b) or (13), the court may make any order that the court sees fit, including without limitation –
@ref Article 20(1) (src:514)
@export Whether the court may make an order of a given kind on an application under Article 19(9)(b) or (13); nothing is owed or permitted before an application is made
GIVEN `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether the Commissioner has applied to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)
      kind IS A `Kind of order under Article 20(1)`
        @desc The kind of order in question
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 20`
`Art 20(1) the court may make the order` `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` kind MEANS
    IF   `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)`
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MAY   `make an order on the application` `the court` kind
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:515 | (a) an order dismissing the application;
@ref Article 20(1)(a) (src:515)
GIVEN `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 20`
`Art 20(1)(a) the court may make an order dismissing the application` `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` MEANS
    `Art 20(1) the court may make the order` `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` `an order dismissing the application`

-- src:516 | (b) an order giving permission for the governor to act as a governor despite a reportable matter;
@ref Article 20(1)(b) (src:516)
GIVEN `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 20`
`Art 20(1)(b) the court may make an order giving permission for the governor to act as a governor` `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` MEANS
    `Art 20(1) the court may make the order` `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` `an order giving permission for the governor to act as a governor despite a reportable matter`

-- src:517 | (c) an order that the entity must not be registered as a charity;
@ref Article 20(1)(c) (src:517)
GIVEN `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 20`
`Art 20(1)(c) the court may make an order that the entity must not be registered` `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` MEANS
    `Art 20(1) the court may make the order` `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` `an order that the entity must not be registered as a charity`

-- src:518 | (d) an order requiring a registered charity to suspend or remove the governor;
@ref Article 20(1)(d) (src:518)
GIVEN `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 20`
`Art 20(1)(d) the court may make an order requiring a registered charity to suspend or remove the governor` `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` MEANS
    `Art 20(1) the court may make the order` `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` `an order requiring a registered charity to suspend or remove the governor`

-- src:519 | (e) an order making any provision that could be made by a required steps notice;
@ref Article 20(1)(e) (src:519)
GIVEN `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 20`
`Art 20(1)(e) the court may make an order making any provision that a required steps notice could make` `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` MEANS
    `Art 20(1) the court may make the order` `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` `an order making any provision that could be made by a required steps notice`

-- src:520 | (f) any order ancillary to any such order.
@ref Article 20(1)(f) (src:520)
GIVEN `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 20`
`Art 20(1)(f) the court may make an order ancillary to any such order` `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` MEANS
    `Art 20(1) the court may make the order` `an application has been made to the court under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` `an order ancillary to any such order`

§§§ `Article 20(2) -- the reach and the terms of an order`

-- (2)(a) An order may apply to a governor in respect of a particular registered charity, a description of registered charities or all registered charities.
-- Whether the charity in question is the particular one, or within the description, is a fact the caller supplies.
-- src:521 | (2) An order under paragraph (1) may, without limitation –
-- src:522 | (a) apply to a governor in respect of a particular registered charity, a description of registered charities or all registered charities;
@ref Article 20(2)(a) (src:521-522)
@export Whether an order of the court applies to a given registered charity: the order is about that charity, or about a description the charity falls within, or about all registered charities
GIVEN order IS AN `Order made by the court under Article 20(1)`
        @desc The order
      `the charity is the particular charity the order names` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether the charity in question is the particular registered charity the order names
      `the charity falls within the description the order names` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether the charity in question falls within the description of registered charities the order names
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 20(2)(a) the order applies to the charity` order `the charity is the particular charity the order names` `the charity falls within the description the order names` MEANS
    CONSIDER order's reach
    WHEN `a particular registered charity` THEN `the charity is the particular charity the order names`
    WHEN `a description of registered charities` THEN `the charity falls within the description the order names`
    WHEN `all registered charities` THEN TRUE

-- (2)(b) An order may be made subject to any condition or time limit that appears to the court to be necessary or equitable. The condition is the court's and an input
-- (whether it has been complied with is asked by the Articles that read the order); the time limit is a last day, and F6.22 says whether that day is inside the order.
-- src:523 | (b) be made subject to any condition or time limit that appears to the court to be necessary or equitable.
@ref Article 20(2)(b) (src:523)
@export Whether an order of the court is in force on a given day: it has been made, and any time limit on it has not ended
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.22 (whether the last day named in a time limit is inside the order)
      order IS AN `Order made by the court under Article 20(1)`
        @desc The order
      day IS A DATE
        @desc The day on which the order is asked about
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 20(2)(b) the order of the court is in force on the day` interp order day MEANS
        order's `made on` AT MOST day
    AND CONSIDER order's `time limit ends on`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE
        WHEN JUST last THEN `the day is within a period whose last day is named` (interp's `whether the last day named in a time limit is inside the permission`) day last

-- src:517 | (c) an order that the entity must not be registered as a charity;
@ref Article 20(1)(c) (src:517)
@export Whether the court has ordered that the entity must not be registered as a charity: some order of that kind is in force on the day; the answer is IP24
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.22 (the last day of a time limit on the order)
      orders IS A LIST OF `Order made by the court under Article 20(1)`
        @desc The orders the court has made on applications concerning the entity
      `the day in question` IS A DATE
        @desc The day on which the question is asked
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 20(1)(c) the court orders that the entity must not be registered` interp orders `the day in question` MEANS
    any (GIVEN order YIELD
                order's kind EQUALS `an order that the entity must not be registered as a charity`
            AND `Art 20(2)(b) the order of the court is in force on the day` interp order `the day in question`)
        orders

§§§ `Article 20(3) -- who may apply for a disqualification order`

-- (3) If it appears to the Commissioner or the Attorney General that it is or may be expedient in the public interest that a person should not, without the leave of the court, be a governor of or
-- in any way be concerned or take part in the management of a registered charity, the Commissioner or the Attorney General may apply to the court for a disqualification order.
-- F6.23: whose view lets which of them apply. The reading taken is that each may apply on its own view; the other lets a view held by either open the way to both.
@ref Article 20(3) (F6.23) (src:524)
GIVEN own IS A BOOLEAN
      other IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F6.23 reading A: only the applicant's own view that it is expedient` own other MEANS
    own

@ref Article 20(3) (F6.23) (src:524)
GIVEN own IS A BOOLEAN
      other IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F6.23 reading B: the view of either the Commissioner or the Attorney General` own other MEANS
    own OR other

@ref Article 20(3) (F6.23) (src:524)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
      own IS A BOOLEAN
      other IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the view of expediency that lets the applicant apply` interp own other MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `whose view of expediency lets a person apply under Article 20(3)`
    WHEN `only the applicant's own view that it is expedient`
        THEN `F6.23 reading A: only the applicant's own view that it is expedient` own other
    WHEN `the view of either the Commissioner or the Attorney General`
        THEN `F6.23 reading B: the view of either the Commissioner or the Attorney General` own other

-- src:524 | (3) If it appears to the Commissioner or the Attorney General that it is or may be expedient in the public interest that a person should not, without the leave of the court, be a governor of or in any way whether directly or indirectly be concerned or take part in the management of a registered charity, the Commissioner or the Attorney General may apply to the court for an order to that effect against the person (a “disqualification order”).
@ref Article 20(3) (src:524)
@export Whether the Commissioner may apply to the court for a disqualification order against a person, on the reading given of whose view of expediency is needed
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.23 (whose view of expediency lets a person apply)
      `it appears to the Commissioner that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc The Commissioner's view
      `it appears to the Attorney General that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc The Attorney General's view
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 20`
`Art 20(3) the Commissioner may apply for a disqualification order` interp `it appears to the Commissioner that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` `it appears to the Attorney General that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` MEANS
    IF   `the view of expediency that lets the applicant apply` interp `it appears to the Commissioner that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` `it appears to the Attorney General that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest`
    THEN PARTY `the Commissioner`
         MAY   `apply to the court for a disqualification order` `the Commissioner`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:524 | (3) If it appears to the Commissioner or the Attorney General that it is or may be expedient in the public interest that a person should not, without the leave of the court, be a governor of or in any way whether directly or indirectly be concerned or take part in the management of a registered charity, the Commissioner or the Attorney General may apply to the court for an order to that effect against the person (a “disqualification order”).
@ref Article 20(3) (src:524)
@export Whether the Attorney General may apply to the court for a disqualification order against a person, on the reading given of whose view of expediency is needed
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.23 (whose view of expediency lets a person apply)
      `it appears to the Commissioner that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc The Commissioner's view
      `it appears to the Attorney General that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc The Attorney General's view
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 20`
`Art 20(3) the Attorney General may apply for a disqualification order` interp `it appears to the Commissioner that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` `it appears to the Attorney General that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` MEANS
    IF   `the view of expediency that lets the applicant apply` interp `it appears to the Attorney General that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` `it appears to the Commissioner that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest`
    THEN PARTY `the Attorney General`
         MAY   `apply to the court for a disqualification order` `the Attorney General`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- Paragraph (3) as one power of two public officers: the applicant names which of them is in question (no other applicant has the power).
-- src:524 | (3) If it appears to the Commissioner or the Attorney General that it is or may be expedient in the public interest that a person should not, without the leave of the court, be a governor of or in any way whether directly or indirectly be concerned or take part in the management of a registered charity, the Commissioner or the Attorney General may apply to the court for an order to that effect against the person (a “disqualification order”).
@ref Article 20(3) (src:524)
@export Whether the Commissioner or the Attorney General, as the applicant in question, may apply to the court for a disqualification order; nobody else has the power
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.23 (whose view of expediency lets a person apply)
      applicant IS A `Legal actor`
        @desc Who would apply: the Commissioner or the Attorney General
      `it appears to the Commissioner that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc The Commissioner's view
      `it appears to the Attorney General that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc The Attorney General's view
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 20`
`Art 20(3) the Commissioner or the Attorney General may apply for a disqualification order` interp applicant `it appears to the Commissioner that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` `it appears to the Attorney General that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` MEANS
    IF   applicant EQUALS `the Commissioner`
    THEN `Art 20(3) the Commissioner may apply for a disqualification order` interp `it appears to the Commissioner that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` `it appears to the Attorney General that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest`
    ELSE IF   applicant EQUALS `the Attorney General`
         THEN `Art 20(3) the Attorney General may apply for a disqualification order` interp `it appears to the Commissioner that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest` `it appears to the Attorney General that a disqualification order is or may be expedient in the public interest`
         ELSE FULFILLED

§§§ `Article 20(4) -- when the court may make a disqualification order`

-- src:526 | (a) the person’s conduct in relation to a registered charity, or to an entity applying for registration; or
@ref Article 20(4)(a) (src:526)
@export Whether the person is unfit to be concerned in the management of a registered charity by virtue of the person's conduct in relation to a registered charity or to an entity applying for registration (the court's satisfaction, supplied as a fact)
GIVEN `the court is satisfied that the person's conduct in relation to a registered charity or an applicant makes the person unfit` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc The court's satisfaction on the person's conduct
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 20(4)(a) the person is unfit by virtue of the person's conduct` `the court is satisfied that the person's conduct in relation to a registered charity or an applicant makes the person unfit` MEANS
    `the court is satisfied that the person's conduct in relation to a registered charity or an applicant makes the person unfit`

-- src:527 | (b) the seriousness of a reportable matter in relation to the person.
@ref Article 20(4)(b) (src:527)
@export Whether the person is unfit to be concerned in the management of a registered charity by virtue of the seriousness of a reportable matter in relation to the person (the court's satisfaction, supplied as a fact)
GIVEN `the court is satisfied that the seriousness of a reportable matter makes the person unfit` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc The court's satisfaction on the seriousness of the reportable matter
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 20(4)(b) the person is unfit by virtue of the seriousness of a reportable matter` `the court is satisfied that the seriousness of a reportable matter makes the person unfit` MEANS
    `the court is satisfied that the seriousness of a reportable matter makes the person unfit`

-- src:525 | (4) The court may, on such an application or on an application under Article 19(9)(b) or (13), make a disqualification order if it is satisfied that the person is unfit to be concerned in the management of a registered charity, by virtue of –
@ref Article 20(4) (src:525)
@export Whether the court may make a disqualification order: on an application under Article 20(3), or under Article 19(9)(b) or (13), if it is satisfied that the person is unfit to be concerned in the management of a registered charity by virtue of the person's conduct or the seriousness of a reportable matter
GIVEN `an application has been made under Article 20(3), or under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether an application of one of those kinds has been made against the person
      `the person is unfit by virtue of the person's conduct` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Article 20(4)(a): the court is satisfied the person is unfit by virtue of the person's conduct
      `the person is unfit by virtue of the seriousness of a reportable matter` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Article 20(4)(b): the court is satisfied the person is unfit by virtue of the seriousness of a reportable matter
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 20`
`Art 20(4) the court may make a disqualification order` `an application has been made under Article 20(3), or under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)` `the person is unfit by virtue of the person's conduct` `the person is unfit by virtue of the seriousness of a reportable matter` MEANS
    IF       `an application has been made under Article 20(3), or under Article 19(9)(b) or (13)`
         AND (   `the person is unfit by virtue of the person's conduct`
              OR `the person is unfit by virtue of the seriousness of a reportable matter`)
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MAY   `make a disqualification order` `the court`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§§ `Article 20(5) -- the period of a disqualification order`

-- (5) A disqualification order is to be for such period, not exceeding 15 years, as the court directs. The period is the court's direction (a first and a last day); the Law fixes only its limit.
-- F6.14: how the fifteen years are counted from the first day. On the reading taken the last day is the anniversary, 29 February falling back to 28 February.
-- src:528 | (5) A disqualification order is to be for such period, not exceeding 15 years, as the court directs.
@ref Article 20(5) (src:528)
@export The latest last day a disqualification order beginning on a given day may have under Article 20(5): fifteen years from its first day, counted on the reading given
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.14 (how the fifteen years are counted)
      begins IS A DATE
        @desc The first day of the period the court directs
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 20(5) the latest last day of a disqualification order beginning on` interp begins MEANS
    `the last day of a period of years` (interp's `how the fifteen years of Article 20(5) are counted`) begins 15

-- src:528 | (5) A disqualification order is to be for such period, not exceeding 15 years, as the court directs.
@ref Article 20(5) (src:528)
@export Whether the period of a disqualification order is within the limit of Article 20(5): it does not run beyond fifteen years from its first day
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.14
      order IS A `Disqualification order`
        @desc The order, with the first and last days of the period the court directs
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 20(5) the order's period is within the limit` interp order MEANS
    order's `last day of the period` AT MOST `Art 20(5) the latest last day of a disqualification order beginning on` interp (order's `begins on`)

-- A disqualification order binds a person from the first to the last day of the period the court directs.
@ref Article 20(5) and (6) (src:528) (src:529)
GIVEN order IS A `Disqualification order`
      day IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the disqualification order is in force on the day` order day MEANS
        order's `begins on` AT MOST day
    AND day AT MOST order's `last day of the period`

§§§ `Article 20(6) -- contravening a disqualification order is an offence`

-- A person contravenes the order by being a governor of, or in any way concerned or taking part in the management of, a registered charity, on a day the order is in force, without the leave of the court.
-- (The content of the order is Article 20(3): "should not, without the leave of the court, be a governor of or in any way whether directly or indirectly be concerned or take part in the management of a registered charity".)
-- src:524 | (3) If it appears to the Commissioner or the Attorney General that it is or may be expedient in the public interest that a person should not, without the leave of the court, be a governor of or in any way whether directly or indirectly be concerned or take part in the management of a registered charity, the Commissioner or the Attorney General may apply to the court for an order to that effect against the person (a “disqualification order”).
-- src:529 | (6) A person who contravenes a disqualification order commits an offence and is liable to imprisonment for 2 years and to a fine.
@ref Article 20(3) and (6) (src:524) (src:529)
@export Whether a person contravenes a disqualification order on a given day: the order binds the person on that day, the person is a governor of or concerned in the management of a registered charity, and the court has not given leave
GIVEN order IS A `Disqualification order`
        @desc The disqualification order
      person IS A STRING
        @desc The name of the person whose conduct is in question
      `the day in question` IS A DATE
        @desc The day on which the person acts
      `the person is a governor of, or concerned or takes part in the management of, a registered charity` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether, on that day, the person is a governor of, or in any way directly or indirectly concerned or taking part in the management of, a registered charity
      `the court has given leave` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether the court has given leave for the person to do so
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 20(6) the person contravenes the disqualification order` order person `the day in question` `the person is a governor of, or concerned or takes part in the management of, a registered charity` `the court has given leave` MEANS
        order's person EQUALS person
    AND `the disqualification order is in force on the day` order `the day in question`
    AND `the person is a governor of, or concerned or takes part in the management of, a registered charity`
    AND NOT `the court has given leave`

-- src:529 | (6) A person who contravenes a disqualification order commits an offence and is liable to imprisonment for 2 years and to a fine.
@ref Article 20(6) (src:529)
@export Whether a person must not, without the leave of the court, be a governor of or be concerned in the management of a registered charity while a disqualification order binds the person; being so is the breach, and an offence under Article 20(6)
GIVEN `the person contravenes the disqualification order` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether the person, on the day in question, is within the order and outside the court's leave (the function Art 20(6) the person contravenes the disqualification order answers this)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 20`
`Art 20(6) a person must not contravene a disqualification order` `the person contravenes the disqualification order` MEANS
    IF   `the person contravenes the disqualification order`
    THEN PARTY `any person`
         SHANT `be a governor of, or be concerned or take part in the management of, a registered charity` `any person`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST  BREACH BY `any person` BECAUSE "Article 20(6): contravened a disqualification order by being a governor of, or concerned or taking part in the management of, a registered charity without the leave of the court; an offence"
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- The prohibition, standing: from the order until its last day, any such act is the failure.
@ref Article 20(6) (src:529)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 20`
`Art 20(6) the standing prohibition of a disqualification order` MEANS
    PARTY `any person`
    SHANT `be a governor of, or be concerned or take part in the management of, a registered charity` `any person`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `any person` BECAUSE "Article 20(6): contravened a disqualification order by being a governor of, or concerned or taking part in the management of, a registered charity without the leave of the court; an offence"

-- The offence is the contravention itself: the paragraph states no excuse.
-- src:529 | (6) A person who contravenes a disqualification order commits an offence and is liable to imprisonment for 2 years and to a fine.
@ref Article 20(6) (src:529)
@export Whether a person commits the offence of Article 20(6): contravenes a disqualification order
GIVEN `the person contravenes the disqualification order` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether the person contravenes the disqualification order on the day in question
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 20(6) the person commits an offence` `the person contravenes the disqualification order` MEANS
    `the person contravenes the disqualification order`

-- The penalty is a ceiling, as data: imprisonment for 2 years (24 months on the reading taken of the unit, F6.16) and a fine for which the Law states no maximum, so none is given.
-- src:529 | (6) A person who contravenes a disqualification order commits an offence and is liable to imprisonment for 2 years and to a fine.
@ref Article 20(6) (src:529)
@export The penalty Article 20(6) provides for the offence: imprisonment for a term of up to 24 months and a fine for which the Law states no maximum; the answer is IP30 for this offence
GIVETH A `Penalty provided`
`Art 20(6) the penalty for contravening a disqualification order` MEANS
    `Penalty provided` WITH
        `imprisonment up to, in months` IS JUST 24
        fine IS `a fine with no maximum stated in the Law`

-- F6.16: "2 years". On the reading taken the term is 24 calendar months from its start; on the other it is 730 days.
@ref Article 20(6) (F6.16) (src:529)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
      start IS A DATE
      days IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 20(6) a term of days from the start is within the maximum of two years` interp start days MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `how a term of imprisonment of one year or two years is measured`
    WHEN `calendar months` THEN (start PLUS days) AT MOST `add months` start 24
    WHEN `days` THEN days AT MOST 730
